<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this in the
    same commit as the tag.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="GeoStamp"
           version="0.1.2"
           display-name-key="GeoStamp_Name"
           description-key="GeoStamp_Desc"
           control-type="standard">

    <!--
      Leave this disabled unless the control calls a third-party service.

      `enabled="true"` makes the control **premium**, and premium is a cost
      somebody else pays: every end user of an app containing this component
      then needs a Power Apps licence rather than an Office 365 one. Nothing
      fails to build, nothing warns at install, and the person who finds out is
      not the person who typed it — so this is worth confirming with whoever
      owns the control rather than flipping to make a `fetch` work.

      When it is genuinely enabled, list every domain. `<domain>` children are
      declarative and can be listed under `enabled="false"` too, which is the
      schema's own second example; it is the `enabled` attribute alone that
      carries the licensing consequence.

          <external-service-usage enabled="true">
            <domain>tile.example.com</domain>
          </external-service-usage>

      `npm run check` fails on `enabled="true"` with no domain, and warns on
      `enabled="true"` at all.

      IT IS ENABLED HERE, AND THAT WAS A DECISION. The optional map thumbnail
      loads a tile from a third-party host, which is exactly what this element
      exists to declare. The cost is real and falls on the installer: an app
      containing this component is a premium app.

      What keeps that honest is that the map is off unless a maker turns it on.
      `showMap` is a TwoOptions input, and a TwoOptions input cannot default to
      on — so the shipped default reaches no external host at all, and every
      other feature of the control works with it off. See docs/limitations.md,
      which says this to the person deciding whether to install it.

      `tileUrlTemplate` lets a maker point at their own tile server instead, in
      which case nothing leaves their own infrastructure. The domain below is
      the default's, not a restriction.
    -->
    <external-service-usage enabled="true">
      <domain>tile.openstreetmap.org</domain>
    </external-service-usage>

    <!--
      PCFHub generates the component's API reference from this file, at the tag
      of each release.

      It records `display-name-key` and `description-key` *verbatim* — it does
      not read the .resx — so a reader sees the key itself unless something
      changes it. Two ways to get readable text: keep the keys themselves
      readable, or correct them once in the hub's admin panel, where editing a
      property locks that field so later syncs leave it alone.

      The platform still resolves these against the .resx for the maker's
      properties pane, so the keys must exist there either way.
    -->
    <!--
      The stamp, as text. Bound and required: this is the column the control is
      put on, and the only output every host can receive.
    -->
    <property name="value"
              display-name-key="value_Name"
              description-key="value_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="true" />

    <!--
      The same fix as numbers, for a maker who wants to query on it.

      Both are bound and BOTH ARE OPTIONAL, which is not the same as saying
      they are unimportant: a maker may map neither, one, or both, and the
      control has to behave in all four cases. An unmapped bound property
      arrives with `raw === null` and is indistinguishable from a mapped column
      that happens to be empty — so what the control keys on is whether it has
      a value to write, and `getOutputs` omits the ones it does not.

      THESE SHIPPED AS of-type="Decimal" AND THAT WAS A BUG, because it made
      the control unbindable on exactly the columns it was designed for.
      Dataverse's own coordinate columns — account.address1_latitude and every
      address1_/address2_/address3_ pair on contact, lead, publisher and the
      rest — are **Double**, which PCF spells FP. A maker opening the form
      designer on an account could not select the column at all: the picker
      only offers columns whose type matches, and Decimal does not match Double.

      So it is a type group, and this is the case type groups are for. See
      "of-type-group costs more than it looks" in the skill for the ones they
      are not.
    -->
    <type-group name="coordinate">
      <!--
        FP first because it is the one that matters: it is what every standard
        Dataverse address column is. Decimal is here because a custom column
        made by a maker who wanted exact arithmetic is a reasonable thing to
        bind, and it costs nothing to accept.

        Whole.None is deliberately NOT here. It would let the designer offer an
        integer column for a coordinate, and the platform would take the
        binding and silently truncate every reading to the nearest degree —
        about 111 km. A type the control cannot honour is worse in the picker
        than absent from it.

        The type-group's real costs, both accepted here: PCFHub's parser
        flattens the group and publishes the type as "Decimal | FP" in the API
        reference, and of-type-group is commonly read as model-driven only.
        Neither hurts this control — the numeric columns are a model-driven
        convenience, and canvas reads the formatted text value.
      -->
      <type>FP</type>
      <type>Decimal</type>
    </type-group>

    <property name="latitude"
              display-name-key="latitude_Name"
              description-key="latitude_Desc"
              of-type-group="coordinate"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <property name="longitude"
              display-name-key="longitude_Name"
              description-key="longitude_Desc"
              of-type-group="coordinate"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      Six places is roughly 0.1 m at the equator, which is finer than any
      consumer GPS resolves — so the digits past it are noise that reads as
      precision. It is configurable because the useful answer depends on what
      the column is for, not because six is often wrong.
    -->
    <property name="coordinatePrecision"
              display-name-key="coordinatePrecision_Name"
              description-key="coordinatePrecision_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="6" />

    <!--
      Metres. A fix worse than this is stamped ANYWAY, and said so.

      Refusing a vague fix is the wrong call: a 1200-metre fix from inside a
      warehouse is a real answer and often the only one available, and a
      control that discards it leaves the user with nothing and no explanation.
      Stamping it silently is the other wrong call. So it stamps, and the
      readout says how far off it might be.
    -->
    <property name="accuracyThreshold"
              display-name-key="accuracyThreshold_Name"
              description-key="accuracyThreshold_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="100" />

    <!--
      The premium half, and the reason it is a TwoOptions.

      A TwoOptions input cannot be defaulted to on — the platform ignores a
      `default-value` of "true" here — which is normally an annoyance and is
      exactly right this once: the feature that costs the installer a licence
      is off until somebody asks for it.
    -->
    <property name="showMap"
              display-name-key="showMap_Name"
              description-key="showMap_Desc"
              of-type="TwoOptions"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      `{z}`, `{x}` and `{y}` are substituted with slippy-map tile coordinates.
      Point it at an internal tile server and no request leaves the tenant.
    -->
    <property name="tileUrlTemplate"
              display-name-key="tileUrlTemplate_Name"
              description-key="tileUrlTemplate_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png" />

    <!--
      The record this control is on, supplied by the maker.

      This looks like it should be unnecessary, and it is the platform's own
      documented answer. `context` deliberately does not carry the record's
      identity, "because [components] need to be supported on multiple surfaces
      where this information may not be available" — the FAQ says to add input
      properties and bind them on the form, entityId to the record's own id
      column and entityName to a static value.

      The control prefers the undocumented `context.mode.contextInfo` when the
      host has it, and falls back to these. Both paths exist because neither is
      sufficient: contextInfo is absent from the type definitions and from
      canvas, and these two are empty unless a maker configured them.

      Only the photo needs them. Coordinates stamp regardless.
    -->
    <property name="recordId"
              display-name-key="recordId_Name"
              description-key="recordId_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <property name="recordEntity"
              display-name-key="recordEntity_Name"
              description-key="recordEntity_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      Outputs, and the first one is the interesting one.

      `OnChange` fires when an output CHANGES, not when the control writes one.
      Two stamps from the same spot produce the same coordinates, so without a
      counter the second one is silent and a Power Fx handler never runs. The
      counter is what makes a repeat observable — the same problem
      pcf-star-rating solved with `?? undefined` and pcf-action-button with
      `PressCount`.
    -->
    <property name="stampCount"
              display-name-key="stampCount_Name"
              description-key="stampCount_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="output" />

    <!--
      The Note the photo was written to, and canvas's only route to it: canvas
      has no WebAPI, so there the photo is never written and this stays empty.
    -->
    <property name="lastNoteId"
              display-name-key="lastNoteId_Name"
              description-key="lastNoteId_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/GeoStamp.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        Every language the control ships.

        A .resx that exists on disk but is not listed here is not shipped, and
        nothing warns — the file is simply never packed, and the control runs
        in English for that locale while the repository looks translated.
        `npm run check` catches both that and a key missing from a language.

        Drop the ones you will not maintain. A stale translation is worse than
        an honest fallback to English, and every key added to 1033 is a key
        owed to each file listed here.

        THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE AND 1033 GOES LAST. `pcf-start` picks the .resx
        by manifest order rather than by locale: it reads whichever file is
        listed *last* and ignores the LCID and the browser language entirely.
        The platform itself ignores the order completely — a real app resolves
        from the user's provisioned language — so `npm start` is the only thing
        in the world that reads this order, and it is ordered for it. Listing
        1033 first is the tidier-looking arrangement and it makes `npm start`
        render the whole control in Japanese, which reads as a broken build.

        The consequence to remember: ADD A NEW LANGUAGE ABOVE THIS LINE, not
        below it. Appending a sixth file after 1033 silently moves the harness
        to that language, and the symptom shows up in a `npm start` session
        nobody connects to a manifest edit.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/GeoStamp.3082.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/GeoStamp.1036.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/GeoStamp.1031.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/GeoStamp.1041.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/GeoStamp.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      EVERY ONE OF THESE IS required="false", AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.

      `required="true"` does not make a feature more available; it makes the
      component fail to load on a host that lacks it. This control runs in four
      places that each lack a different subset:

        model-driven, mobile   everything works
        model-driven, web      NO GEOLOCATION — getCurrentPosition is documented
                               for canvas and the mobile client only. The web
                               form gets the manual path and the map.
        canvas                 no WebAPI at all, so no photo. Location works.
        Power Pages            no Device API, no Utility, and uses-feature must
                               not be "true" anywhere — which alone settles this.

      So the control feature-detects all four and degrades in four different
      directions rather than refusing to load in three of them. Every branch is
      a switch in dev/host.js and an assertion in dev/smoke.js.

      Utility is here for one thing: `@odata.bind` needs the parent's entity
      SET name, which only `getEntityMetadata` knows.
    -->
    <feature-usage>
      <uses-feature name="Device.getCurrentPosition" required="false" />
      <uses-feature name="Device.captureImage" required="false" />
      <uses-feature name="WebAPI" required="false" />
      <uses-feature name="Utility" required="false" />
    </feature-usage>
  </control>
</manifest>
